Native FIDL bindings for 5BSD
===========================

Status: experimental, tested native C++ backend and generated C ABI. This is
not the Fuchsia WireClient runtime and does not replace production service
protocols. It combines upstream fidlc/fidlgen_cpp natural types and codecs
with native libchannel. No Zircon kernel, syscall emulation, async dispatcher,
or Component Manager is linked. The small imported Zircon headers provide
wire constants and error definitions, not authority.

Build from /usr/src:

  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-runtime.py --test
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-runtime.py --test --sanitize

First build the compiler and generators with the commands in
 tools/build/fuchsia/README. Runtime compilation requires C++20, Python 3.12,
and the imported host tools. Objects, generated sources, the static archive
libfidl_bsd.a, and test executables are under /usr/obj/5bsd-fidl-runtime.
The sanitizer variant uses a separate sanitized/ directory. The native
kernel capability channel is required; the runner fails rather than skipping
its absence. Native library/header/manual targets and the libfidl package are
registered; full world/package and provider-migration gates remain open.

To generate another library, compile its schemas with fidlc --json, then:

  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/generate-bsd.py \
      --json /usr/obj/my-library.json --root /usr/obj/my-bindings --c

The --c option additionally emits the checked C bridge for supported payloads.
Include <fidl/LIBRARY/cpp/bsd.h> or <fidl/LIBRARY/c/bsd.h>. Compile generated
natural_types.cc, common_types.cc and the C bridge bsd.cc as C++20. The public C header contains
no C++ declarations and the application can compile as C11; link the final
program using the libraries reported by pkgconf. Native builds provide libfidl_bsd.so.1 and a
static archive; libchannel is linked separately. The build runner demonstrates
this with c_binding_test.c. Do not include Fuchsia's generated fidl.h: those
messaging APIs require the unported Fuchsia dispatcher and are not the native
backend's interface.

Sharing schema libraries
------------------------
Pass --dependency-json PATH once per dependency-library compiler JSON file,
including transitive dependencies. The adapter validates the graph and
available imported-declaration/layout metadata, then generates each supplied
library in dependency order. Use IR from the same coherent schema build;
import metadata does not encode every semantic compatibility rule.
The intrinsic zx.Handle mapping does not require a separate zx library IR.

  fidlc --json common.json --files common.fidl
  fidlc --json service.json --files common.fidl --files service.fidl
  fidlgen-bsd --json service.json --dependency-json common.json \
      --root generated --c

A types-only library exports its C type declarations too. Each C type belongs
to its declaring library; dependent headers include that library's c/bsd.h.
Compile every emitted cpp/natural_types.cc, cpp/common_types.cc and c/bsd.cc
once per library. The generated c/bridge.h contains private inline C++
conversions shared between libraries; C applications include c/bsd.h instead.
Composed protocols retain their original method ordinals and payload types.
Rejected graphs or schemas leave previously generated files intact.
lib/libfidl/tests/library_test.py exercises three libraries, including a
composed client talking to the original base-protocol server. The same test
runs with the staged SDK and extracted pkgbase payloads.

Binary-facing API
-----------------
The native library builds with `make -C lib/libfidl -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu)`.
Public headers install under /usr/include/fidl/bsd; pinned codec support
headers are isolated under /usr/include/fidl/support. The libfidl-dev package
includes fidl.pc. The optional host SDK installs fidlc, fidl-format,
fidl-lint and fidlgen-bsd, with its private generator under /usr/libexec/fidl.
Build/bootstrap and staging targets live in tools/build/fuchsia/Makefile.
They use the existing pinned imports and do not fetch on install.
Host tools require Python 3.12 and RE2; generated binaries do not.
The optional tools subpackage is selected with FIDL_SDK=yes only after staging
that SDK; it is not required for a normal world build. Release packaging and
external dependency validation remain a maturity gate. After installation, obtain include/link flags with
`pkgconf --cflags --libs fidl`. Generated C applications compile as C11; their
generated bridge compiles as C++20. Link the bridge and generated natural types
with the application. This C ABI does not require a C++ application source.

`test-installed.py` creates a disposable /usr/obj staging root, uses the native
install rules, and compiles/links/runs C11 and C++ consumers against that root.
The test generates bindings using the staged compiler and matching adapter,
then compiles and runs C11/C++ binaries with shared and static native runtimes.
Dependency files must contain no source-tree headers; dynamic dependency
checks distinguish shared from static linkage. Package ownership is recorded
in METALOG. The support-header manifest follows the source include closure
from native public headers and supported generator templates; unrelated
Fuchsia dispatcher headers are not installed.
It neither installs into / nor activates an upgraded service.

FIDL_BSD_ABI_VERSION and fidl_bsd_runtime_abi_version identify the C runtime
ABI. fidl_bsd_connection_create_with_options accepts a sized options structure
for pending-request and outgoing queue limits; initialize it with
FIDL_BSD_OPTIONS_INITIALIZER. Unknown sizes/reserved bits fail with EINVAL.
The original create function uses defaults. Failed creation leaves the fd with
the caller and clears the result pointer. fidl_bsd_connection_peer_epitaph
returns ENOENT until a peer epitaph arrives, then returns its signed status.

Generation uses the explicit upstream -native-bsd mode and upstream-derived
name metadata; it no longer edits generated C++ source. Unsupported schemas
leave prior outputs intact. Accepted fixture implementations are compiled,
linked, and regenerated identically, in addition to public C-header checks.

Ownership and event handling
----------------------------
Connection::Create / fidl_bsd_connection_create take the native endpoint FD
only on success. One thread owns dispatch and all operations on a connection.
Poll uses native kqueue; applications with an existing event loop can register
fd(), use wants_write() to manage write readiness, and call Dispatch().
Close is safe from a callback; destroying the Connection object from its own
callback is not supported. Incoming request contexts may be retained for
asynchronous replies. On the C side retain/release fidl_bsd_request explicitly.
Context retention does not extend borrowed decoded buffers or descriptors.

C++ Descriptor is move-only and uses -1 for absence; descriptor 0 is valid.
The internal codec representation is fd+1 so it can use upstream's absent
slot marker. Those numbers are never encoded as authority: wire bytes carry
only FIDL's handle-presence marker, while native channel attachments carry
actual descriptors. C++ send arguments consume their Descriptor fields even
when encoding or sending fails. Encoded packets own attachments, libchannel
borrows them for immediate sends or makes protected duplicates when queueing,
and packet destruction closes the original owned descriptors.

C outgoing buffers and descriptors are borrowed. The bridge duplicates FDs
with the same close-on-exec/fork locks used by libchannel, then applies the
C++ ownership rules to those duplicates. The original C descriptor remains
owned by the caller and retains its rights. Transfer state remains enforced
by the kernel; copying a descriptor is not a right to increase its ceiling.

A C caller that no longer needs a descriptor passes fidl_bsd_fd_owned(fd)
in the field instead (a value below -1; -1 stays "absent"). The call takes
that descriptor exactly as a C++ Descriptor or ClientEnd/ServerEnd argument
is taken: it is moved into the message with CHANNEL_SEND_MOVE and no
duplicate is made, and the caller must not use or close it afterwards,
whatever the result. The outcomes are exact:
- the call returns an error before sending (a rejected argument, an encode
  error, a closed or shut-down connection, the pending-request or queue
  limit, a kernel refusal such as ENOTCAPABLE for CAP_XFER_NONE or EINVAL
  for a non-passable object): the call has closed the descriptor;
- the message is accepted (sent at once, or queued because the channel is
  busy): the runtime owns the descriptor; the kernel closes the moved
  original on delivery, and libchannel closes a queued copy when the send
  fails later, is cancelled, or the connection dies;
- the call succeeds: the caller's descriptor number is already closed.
Borrowed and owned descriptors may be mixed in one message, including inside
vectors, arrays, tables, unions and boxed structs: each generated bridge
holds a release pass (CWRelease_TYPE in c/bridge.h) that a failed call runs
over the whole C value, closing every owned descriptor the encoder had not
yet attached (the packet closes those it had). Existing callers, who pass
plain descriptors, are unaffected. Received descriptor fields are never
encoded this way; they are plain descriptors or -1.

Decoded C callback buffers last only until callback return. Descriptor fields
are also released then unless fidl_bsd_take_fd(&value->fd) is used to take
ownership; it replaces the field with -1. Do not close a borrowed descriptor
or overwrite it with another FD. Take it first. Copy strings before retaining
them. C++ callbacks receive owning generated values. A retained Incoming or
C request context still exposes the original kernel-stamped native message.
Neither a peer UID/PID nor a message field grants authorization.

Allocation
----------
Steady-state calls do not allocate and copy the body once. A connection
encodes every request, reply and event straight into a send buffer reserved
from libchannel (channel_outgoing_reserve(3), 4 KiB, the size libchannel
recycles per channel): the wire codec or the natural encoder's copy writes
where the kernel reads, the commit sends from there or makes the buffer the
queue entry without copying, a failed send returns the reservation (the
packet still owns its descriptors and closes them), and a body over 4 KiB
spills into the packet's own storage and goes through the copying send as
before. Three bounded slabs recycle its per-call objects: the client's
outstanding-call records, received message objects (Incoming, which generated
servers and clients receive as std::unique_ptr), and the C request contexts
(fidl_bsd_request). Each slab is sized from the connection's
max_pending_requests option (256 by default) and grows in chunks of eight
only up to that bound; beyond it, or when a chunk cannot be allocated, the
object comes from the heap exactly as before, so ENOMEM behaviour is
unchanged (an allocation failure closes the connection with ENOMEM and every
attached descriptor). A retained request context or Incoming may outlive its
connection; it keeps the slab alive and returns its block on release. C
decoded views live in a 2 KiB on-stack arena per message and use the heap
only beyond it. What still allocates per call is outside the runtime: the
natural C++ types themselves (a std::string longer than its inline
capacity), and libchannel's output queue when a send cannot complete at
once. ipc_bench (tests/ipc_bench.cc, `make -C tools/build/fuchsia
check-bench`) counts these through link-time wrappers.

All C status functions return errno values directly (zero means success).
The fd accessor returns a borrowed descriptor or -1. C++ uses fit::result
with errno errors for calls and errno values for other operations. Callbacks
complete pending calls once, including cancellation and shutdown. Cancellation
is local; it does not undo remote work. Late replies and their descriptors
are discarded by libchannel. Each pending call checks both the FIDL txid and
method ordinal after native token matching. Native 64-bit reply tokens are
never truncated to FIDL's 32-bit IDs; IDs are never reused on a connection.
Reconnect if the ID space is exhausted.

Shutdown sends an epitaph and stops new server sends/replies. Continue polling
until the peer observes it and closes, or apply an application deadline then
Close. Sending is not a delivery guarantee; immediately closing can discard
queued output. Remote epitaph status is available from peer_epitaph(). An
unknown strict interaction or malformed message closes the connection. Providers must
still implement application authorization, quotas, and retry/idempotency rules.

Protocol evolution
------------------
Closed protocols reject all unknown interactions. Ajar protocols accept only
unknown flexible one-way methods/events. Open protocols additionally answer
unknown flexible two-way calls with the standard FIDL unknown-method result.
The generated client reports that result as EOPNOTSUPP without closing the
connection. C callbacks receive a null result pointer in this case. Application
error results remain separate from callback errno status.

Generated C server/event handler structures for open/ajar protocols expose an
optional unknown_interaction callback (ordinal, plus two-way flag for servers).
C++ handlers provide OnUnknownMethod/OnUnknownEvent hooks. Unknown attachments
are closed by the runtime; these hooks receive no payload or capabilities.
Known interactions ignore the sender's strict/flexible flag for compatibility.
Creating a generated client supplies a default event handler when none exists;
known unhandled events are decoded and discarded, and unknown events follow
the protocol's openness rules. In C this occurs on the first generated client
call; bind event handlers explicitly to receive events before making a call.
Explicitly bound event handlers take precedence.

Versioning
----------
Every production library (the fifteen bsd.<name>.v1 libraries under lib/) is
versioned with FIDL availability under one platform, "bsd":

  @available(platform="bsd", added=1)
  library bsd.network.v1;

The platform is written explicitly so that it does not silently follow a
library rename (fidlc would otherwise derive it from the first name
component). The library name's .vN suffix is the wire major: ordinals and
layouts are a stable contract, and an incompatible change is a new library
name. The platform version bsd:N is the additive level shared by every bsd.*
library: a method, event, table field, flexible union variant or enum/bits
member added later is marked @available(added=N) with the next level, and
one level covers the whole platform (a library with nothing new at level N
is unchanged there). Removals and @available(deprecated=...) are not used;
retiring something is a new wire major.

fidlc refuses to compile a versioned library without a selected version
(fi-0201), so every compile of a bsd.* schema passes --available bsd:N:
lib/libfidl/fidl-bindings.mk compiles every binding set at FIDL_AVAILABLE
(default bsd:1; a FIDL_FLAGS.NAME that names --available itself, an
evolution test's bsd:2, takes precedence), fidlgen-build.py applies the same
default (--available, empty for none), and anyone compiling an installed
/usr/share/fidl/*.fidl schema by hand passes `--available bsd:1`. Changing
FIDL_AVAILABLE alone does not regenerate an existing set; clean first.
Peers built at different levels interoperate under the protocol-evolution
rules above: lib/libfidl/tests/evolution compiles one schema at bsd:1 and
bsd:2, proves that the elements added at 2 are absent from the bsd:1 IR and
C header and present at bsd:2, and runs the separately built C peers against
each other in all pairings; tools/build/fuchsia/test-bindings.py proves each
provider's next schema is its production schema at bsd:1. The zx shim and
the test-only libraries (bsd.runtime.test, cross.*) are not versioned.

Supported and unsupported features
----------------------------------
The C/C++ adapters support closed, ajar, and open protocols with strict or
flexible methods and events as permitted by the FIDL compiler. Payloads use local or imported structs/tables/unions, with
upstream C++ name transformations. Anonymous and payload-free methods work.
Upstream natural codecs implement their value types, bounds, padding, UTF-8,
and resource envelopes. The native wrapper also enforces the kernel's 14,336
byte / 32 attachment limit, even where an upstream schema permits more.
The C projection supports local/imported structs, tables, unions, enums/bits, nested
arrays/vectors, optional strings/vectors and boxed types, descriptors, and
protocol endpoints. Strict application-error replies expose their generated
result union; callback errno status remains reserved for transport/conversion
errors. C is the primary application API.
C++ ClientEnd<Protocol> and ServerEnd<Protocol> own native channel descriptors;
C exposes them as owned/borrowed FD fields under the same rules as handles.
Optional endpoints use an empty wrapper or -1, not descriptor zero.
C source member names are preserved where possible. Keywords and size-field
collisions gain a deterministic _fidl suffix, repeated until unambiguous.
The C++ getter name always comes from upstream name metadata. Container
wrappers use readable layout-derived names, with a hash suffix only for very
long nested type names. Missing dependency IR fails generation.

C layout/ownership rules:
- Strings use a pointer plus NAME_size; vectors use a mutable element pointer
  plus NAME_size. String/vector elements in other containers have a generated
  wrapper with data/data_size fields. Arrays are inline C arrays.
- Optional strings/vectors add NAME_present. An absent input must have a null
  pointer and zero size. A present empty value sets present=true and size=0.
- Boxed structs and optional unions use nullable pointers. Tables use has_NAME
  flags independently of their member's optional-value presence.
- Unions use an ordinal tag and a value union, with generated NAME_MEMBER_tag
  constants. An unknown flexible union decodes with NAME_unknown_tag; unknown
  data is discarded by the codec and cannot be sent back as a known value.
- Enum/bits values have fixed-width C typedefs and prefixed value constants.
  The upstream codec validates strict values and preserves flexible values.
- Every nested callback buffer lasts only through the callback. Taking a
  nested descriptor transfers ownership exactly as taking a top-level one.
  Retaining the request context does not retain these buffers.
- Conversion bounds allocation work, recursion and descriptor count before
  encoding. Cyclic boxed C input fails rather than overflowing the stack.
  A response-view allocation failure completes the C callback once with
  ENOMEM and releases unclaimed descriptors.

zx.Handle fields map to native Descriptor values; supported subtypes are
NONE (a generic FD), SOCKET (a native socket), and CHANNEL (a native capability
channel). Unsupported Zircon object subtypes fail generation. READ and WRITE
are explicitly mapped to CAP_READ and CAP_WRITE; masks are not cast between
platforms. Explicit socket rights also require/preserve CAP_FSTAT for type
validation. CHANNEL requires SAME_RIGHTS with any native attenuation performed
beforehand. Type verification uses the actual FD; a socket needs fstat rights,
and a channel needs GETINFO permission. SAME_RIGHTS preserves already attenuated
rights and does not grant new ones. Descriptor transfer ceilings, revocation,
and locked exec/fork flags remain kernel policy.

Rust runtime bindings remain an implementation gap, not a permanent architectural restriction.
Add them with generated round-trip and rejection tests before accepting them
in the generator. Full upstream conformance and performance parity are not
claimed. Zircon-only object types and Fuchsia component routing should only
be introduced where a real native capability contract exists; no fake rights
or objects should be added just to make source compile.

Tests
-----
The runtime suite uses actual kernel channels and generated types/client/server
adapters. It covers capability-mode process exchange, descriptor zero, rights
attenuation and transfer ceilings, wrong types, transfer-once/no-transfer,
malformed headers/padding/UTF-8/booleans/attachments, unknown resource envelopes,
typed endpoint exchange/rejection, encoder/decoder allocation failures,
request correlation, reordering, cancellation and late replies, queued-FD
cleanup, peer death, callback shutdown/exceptions, and epitaphs. Deterministic
byte and resource mutation corpora run under the same codecs.

The C11 test covers calls, replies, one-way messages, events, borrowed outbound
FDs, owned outbound FDs (fidl_bsd_fd_owned: success closes the caller's
descriptor; argument, immediate and queued send failures, a non-passable
kqueue and a CAP_XFER_NONE descriptor are all consumed; mixed owned and
borrowed descriptors in one message), taking a received descriptor, deferred
replies, cancellation, invalid buffers/bounds, and shutdown. The C++ suite
repeats the owned-descriptor cases through the C API and checks the slabs:
no allocation across hundreds of steady-state C and C++ calls, 200
outstanding calls at the bound with the next rejected and no growth on the
second round, heap fallback beyond the bound, and allocation-fault injection
at each slab's chunk allocation. c_wire_test checks the owned encode against
the natural codec and the release pass of a failed encode. ASan and UBSan run the native suites. This host's
LeakSanitizer is unavailable; detect_leaks is explicitly disabled and not
reported as a passed check. Descriptor cleanup is checked by pipe EOF after
all intended owners are gone, including rejection and backpressure cases.

Ongoing maturity work and acceptance gates: lib/libfidl/MATURITY.

The native source-tree build supports LIBADD=fidl_bsd for C programs, including
fully static linkage. The dependency declaration includes the C++ runtime;
applications do not need to guess extra C++ ABI libraries. The installed API
test includes a C-only program built through these native make rules.

Native test and package integration
----------------------------------
MK_TESTS builds tests/ through bsd.test.mk and installs its Kyuafile and test
executables under /usr/tests/lib/libfidl. The C11 integration test is always
included; MK_GOOGLETEST controls the C++ runtime suite. libfidl-tests packages
these tests, with a debug subpackage when split debug files are enabled.
The binaries link statically so allocation-failure injection covers the runtime;
no FIDL host compiler, Python, Go, or source tree is needed to run them.
Run `kyua test -k /usr/tests/lib/libfidl/Kyuafile` on a matching 5BSD kernel.

The runtime fixtures (tests/runtime.fidl) and every provider's bindings are
generated into the object tree during buildworld (lib/libfidl/fidl-bindings.mk:
a bootstrap fidlc plus the Python generator in tools/build/fuchsia; Python
3.12 is a build-only prerequisite, never installed). Nothing generated is
checked in. `make -C tools/build/fuchsia check-bindings` generates every set
twice and rejects any difference.

`make -C tools/build/fuchsia check-native` runs native and sanitizer suites,
staged C/C++ SDK consumers, bsd.test.mk/Kyua tests, and pkgbase archive checks.
It requires the already bootstrapped host tools and Kyua. The installed check
uses the running host's base libraries, plus freshly built FIDL/libchannel;
world builds use the target libraries and must run on their matching kernel.
`test-installed.py --packages` stages and verifies the SDK and native tests,
then tests their extracted packages without installing anything on the host.
Full world/release validation, conformance and provider migration still apply.

BSDTime and libtimecmp are the first source-tree C provider/client migration.
Their native host-safe tests use the actual FIDL handlers with simulated clock
gates; test-provider.py is included in check-native. Public C ABI 1 is retained;
source wire framing changes to bsd.time.v1 with explicit version negotiation.
Production activation, real-gate VM and coordinated rollback remain required.
(Superseded 2026-10-22: fourteen of the fifteen capability daemons serve FIDL,
including every system.Network endpoint; see tools/build/fuchsia/DAEMONS.md.)

The provider package check, test-provider.py --packages, also validates split
manual/debug packages and the registered bsdtime-tests package. Extracted
payloads pass native tests and C/C++ API probes; debug-link CRCs and staged-file
ownership are checked. Its runtime archive contains only BSDTime/BSDTimectl,
not a deployable complete world. See tools/build/fuchsia/README.
